issue(id="0007" status="open")
  title Improve HTML-to-Pug import fidelity and trimming controls
  user-story
    as-a maintainer of the puggers HTML converter
    i-want imports to preserve more meaningful structure and text without losing the current ergonomic defaults
    so-that converted output is a better foundation for real editing and later formatting
  premise.
    The HTML converter is already useful, but it currently makes a few strong
    simplifications: it removes all attributes unless allowlisted, collapses
    normal text whitespace aggressively, uses a short raw-text tag whitelist,
    and only has a basic outer-document trimming switch. Those defaults are good
    for a first pass, but fidelity-oriented users will likely want a richer set
    of import controls once the formatter grows more capable.
  acceptance-criteria
    criterion Evaluate and document which whitespace transformations in HTML import are intentional defaults versus candidates for configurable preservation.
    criterion Add configurable front-matter and back-matter trimming or equivalent region-selection controls if that remains a confirmed need.
    criterion Revisit the collapse-single-nested heuristic and define when wrapper removal is considered safe.
    criterion Add tests for higher-fidelity conversion scenarios, especially around normal text, comments, and nested wrappers.
  review-items
    item(status="open") Decide whether the converter should expose fidelity presets such as aggressive normalization versus preserve-more structure.
    item(status="open") Confirm whether imported comments should always be kept by default now that tasks and docs may treat them as authored content.
    item(status="open") Keep the API shared enough that future npm wrapping does not need a separate behavior matrix.

